  </nav>

  <!-- Article Header -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 shadow-xs not-prose">
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-blue-50 text-blue-700 text-xs font-semibold mb-3 border border-blue-100">
      <span>📐 Architectural Millwork &amp; Layout Math</span>
      <span>•</span>
      <span>Symmetry &amp; Proportions</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">
      Board and Batten Spacing Formula: How Far Apart to Space Battens
    </h1>
    <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
      Whether you are installing an interior dining room accent wall or exterior siding on a modern farmhouse, nothing ruins the aesthetic faster than ending at a corner with an awkward, narrow 2-inch gap. Here is the mathematical formula used by master trim carpenters to achieve perfectly equal batten spacing.
    </p>

    <!-- Quick Stats Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-6 border-t border-gray-100">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Standard Exterior Spacing</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">12" or 16" On-Center</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Interior Accent Spacing</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-emerald-600 mt-0.5">10" to 14" On-Center</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500">Common Batten Width</div>
        <div class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900 mt-0.5">1x2 (1.5") or 1x3 (2.5")</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 sm:p-8 mb-8 prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">The Universal Batten Spacing Formula</h2>
    <p>
      To find the exact distance between the inside edges of adjacent batten strips, you need three numbers:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Wall Width ($W$):</strong> Total wall length in inches (e.g., 144 inches for a 12-foot wall).</li>
      <li><strong>Batten Width ($B$):</strong> Actual width of your trim strip (e.g., 2.5 inches for a nominal 1x3).</li>
      <li><strong>Desired Target Spacing ($S$):</strong> Target opening between battens (typically ~12 inches).</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-gray-200 my-6 not-prose">
      <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-blue-900 uppercase tracking-wide mb-3">Step-by-Step Calculation Example</h3>
      <div class="space-y-3 font-mono text-xs text-gray-800">
        <p><strong>Step 1: Estimate Number of Spaces:</strong><br />Spaces = Wall Width &divide; (Target Spacing + Batten Width)<br />Spaces = 144" &divide; (12" + 2.5") = 144 &divide; 14.5 = 9.93 &rarr; <strong>Round to 10 Spaces</strong></p>
        <p><strong>Step 2: Calculate Number of Battens:</strong><br />If ending with corner boards: Battens = Spaces - 1 = 10 - 1 = <strong>9 Battens</strong></p>
        <p><strong>Step 3: Total Batten Width:</strong><br />9 Battens &times; 2.5" = <strong>22.5 inches</strong> of total wood</p>
        <p><strong>Step 4: Remaining Open Space:</strong><br />144" - 22.5" = <strong>121.5 inches</strong> of open wall</p>
        <p><strong>Step 5: Exact Gap Between Battens:</strong><br />121.5" &divide; 10 Spaces = <strong>12.15 inches (12-1/8")</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-4 p-3 bg-emerald-50 text-emerald-800 rounded-lg text-xs font-sans font-medium border border-emerald-200">
        ✓ Result: Cut a spacer scrap block to exactly <strong>12-1/8 inches</strong>. Place it between each batten for a 100% foolproof, identical reveal across the entire wall!
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">Handling Windows, Doors &amp; Wall Obstacles</h2>
    <p>
      Real walls have windows, doorways, and electrical outlets. When placing battens around openings, use the <strong>Center-Out Layout Method</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Center on Main Features:</strong> Place a batten directly on the centerline of a feature window or fireplace mantel, then measure outward in both directions using your calculated spacing.</li>
      <li><strong>Frame Window Casings:</strong> Ensure your layout does not place a batten 1/2 inch away from a window casing. If a batten falls awkwardly close, widen or narrow your target spacing by 1/2 inch to clear the window trim neatly.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Jobsite Field Note -->
  <FieldNote
    author="Elena Rostova, CBO"
    role="Certified Building Official & Material Takeoff Analyst"
    experience="18+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-800"
    initials="ER"
    title="The 'Cut Block' Carpenter's Trick"
    note="Never use a tape measure to mark every single batten location on a 20-foot wall. Measuring and pencil-marking 15 separate times introduces cumulative layout errors; a 1/16-inch variance on each mark results in your last space being off by nearly an inch! Instead, calculate your exact spacing (say, 12-1/8 inches), go to your miter saw, and cut a 1x4 scrap board to exactly 12-1/8 inches. Hold that wood block against the previous batten, slide your next batten tight against the block, check for plumb with a 4-foot level, and nail. Every single section will be mathematically identical in a fraction of the time."
  />

  <!-- Costly Mistakes -->
  <CostlyMistakes
    tradeName="Board & Batten Layout"
    mistakes={[
      {
        number: 1,
        title: "Starting with a Full Space from One Corner",
        costImpact: "$450 (Asymmetrical Wall & Redo)",
        description: "Starting on the left corner with a 12-inch gap and working blindly across usually leaves a weird 3-inch or 14-inch gap at the opposite right corner.",
        solution: "Always perform the full wall width calculation first, or layout from the center of the wall working symmetrically toward both corners."
      },
      {
        number: 2,
        title: "Trusting Walls and Corners to be Plumb",
        costImpact: "$600 (Crooked, Leaning Batten Grid)",
        description: "Drywall corners in residential homes are almost never plumb. If you measure off an out-of-plumb corner, every subsequent batten will lean at an angle.",
        solution: "Use a laser level or 6-foot Stabila level to plumb every single vertical batten independently before driving finish nails."
      },
      {
        number: 3,
        title: "Nailing Directly Into Drywall Without Construction Adhesive",
        costImpact: "$300 (Popped Battens & Warping)",
        description: "Battens that do not hit wooden wall studs have very little holding power in 1/2\" drywall; finish brad nails will pull out when humidity changes.",
        solution: "Apply a small serpentine bead of Loctite Power Grab construction adhesive to the back of every batten, in addition to shooting 18-gauge brad nails at opposing 45-degree angles."
      }
    ]}
  />

  <!-- Builder Community Reviews -->
  <CommunityReviews
    tradeTitle="Batten Spacing Guide"
    reviews={[
      {
        author: "Hannah & Josh L.",
        location: "Grand Rapids, MI",
        projectScope: "Master Bedroom Headboard Accent Wall",
        savingsClaim: "Saved $850 vs Trim Carpenter",
        feedback: "The spacer block trick saved our sanity! Our wall was 158 inches wide. We used 1x3 MDF boards and ended up with exactly 11.4 inches between each batten. Cut a spacer block, taped it with painter's tape, and knocked out the wall in one Saturday afternoon.",
        keyTakeaway: "Paint the backing wall with 2 coats of your wall color before nailing up the battens; cutting-in with a paintbrush between 15 battens takes forever."
      },
      {
        author: "Marcus P.",
        location: "Charlotte, NC",
        projectScope: "Modern Farmhouse Living Room Wainsoting",
        savingsClaim: "Zero Awkward Corner Slivers",
        feedback: "Used the center-out method because we had two large windows. Adjusted the spacing by 3/8 inch so the battens flanked each window casing with a perfect 3-inch reveal. Looked custom-built.",
        keyTakeaway: "Use caulk on all side seams and spackle on the nail holes before priming for a seamless look."
      }
    ]}
  />

